# texture_bilerp — the TEXTURE.TMU bilinear channel, proved on the module
# zhao_texture_tmu actually instantiates four times per filtered sample
# (ZH-027). Run with cwd = tests/formal.
#
# TWO TASKS, both required:
#   bmc   — P1..P4: the output is EXACTLY the derived single-rounded weighted
#           sum (which catches all three named one-LSB traps at once, and --
#           because the wide law is compared against an EIGHT-BIT output --
#           also proves the sum can never leave the field); the weights are a
#           partition of unity, Sw = 65,536 exactly, which with P1 makes a
#           flat footprint filter to itself; fu = fv = 0 is the exact identity
#           on t00, which is what makes nearest sampling the same datapath;
#           and the 8-bit field holds with no clamp in the datapath. All are
#           unconditional over free inputs, so depth 2 is the whole property:
#           the harness is combinational and its inputs are unconstrained.
#
# WHAT WAS WRITTEN, RUN, AND REMOVED because it does not close on this engine
# -- recorded so nobody re-adds it blind. CONVEXITY (min(t) <= out <= max(t))
# is genuinely nonlinear: the solver would have to know t_i*w_i <= max*w_i,
# which bit-blasting does not get cheaply. MONOTONICITY in fu needs a second
# DUT instance, doubling the multipliers. Measured here (boolector, depth 2,
# 32-bit law lane): the exactness theorem alone closes in 339 s, the four
# shipped laws in 741 s standalone and 1,264 s as the CTest lane -- which is
# why that lane carries TIMEOUT 3600 rather than the 2100 the other formal
# lanes use. With convexity added the task ran past 25 minutes twice without
# an answer. The gap is named in the harness header rather than assumed away.
#
#   cover — the corners the comments claim are REACHABLE: an EXACT ROUNDING
#           TIE firing (without it, the exactness theorem also holds for a
#           truncating filter on every non-tie input, which is almost all of
#           them), the result landing strictly INSIDE its own footprint
#           (without which P1 would also hold for a filter that never left
#           tap 0), both far corners, the 128/128 centre, and a flat footprint
#           at a non-trivial fraction filtering to itself.
#
# SCOPE-TOTAL: depth 2 is the FULL state space, not a bound. The harness is
# purely combinational (zhao_texture_bilerp is three always_comb blocks; the
# only always_ff blocks hold the assert and cover statements) and every input
# is unconstrained, so the reachable state set is identical at every step. The
# input parametrisation is likewise total rather than sampled: the four texel
# bytes are u8 because every texture format in charter 15 decodes to charter
# 8's 8-bit channel lanes, and the two fractions are u8 because they ARE
# unit8s (spec/qformats.md 2). Free (t00,t10,t01,t11,fu,fv) is all 2^48 inputs
# the block can ever be handed. There is no horizon for a scope guard.
#
# WHAT THIS DOES NOT PROVE, stated plainly. The address generation, the three
# wrap folds, the mip level selection and its base-4-repunit offset, the five
# format decodes, the palette pass, the cache handshake, the mode-error rules
# and the state machine are NOT proved here: they are covered by the
# differential lanes against zref::Tmu (texture_tmu_directed and
# texture_tmu_random) and by the mutation evidence in
# design/contracts/TEXTURE.TMU.md. What IS proved is the arithmetic every
# filtered texel in the machine flows through, four times over.
#
# Frontend: read_slang (the lane choice since W2.3). No package dependencies:
# zhao_texture_bilerp.sv is self-contained, so nothing is staged or copied.

[tasks]
bmc
cover

[options]
bmc: mode bmc
bmc: depth 2
cover: mode cover
cover: depth 2
expect pass

[engines]
smtbmc boolector

[script]
read_slang --top texture_bilerp_fv zhao_texture_bilerp.sv texture_bilerp_fv.sv
prep -top texture_bilerp_fv

[files]
../../fpga/rtl/texture/zhao_texture_bilerp.sv
texture_bilerp_fv.sv
